{:edb/db-before-t 2187 :edb/db-after-t 2188 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535500 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:18:55.536Z" 13194139535500 true] [17592186054667 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352328 13194139535500 true] [17592186054667 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352333 13194139535500 true] [17592186054668 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352314 13194139535500 true] [17592186054668 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352321 13194139535500 true] [17592186055211 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535500 true] [17592186055211 1100 "For which values of $x$ is $x^{2} - 5x > 0$?" 13194139535500 false] [17592186055211 1100 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-5x>0?$" 13194139535500 true] [17592186055211 1145 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x & >0 \\\\ x(x-5) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ $(0,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x-5)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x-5)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ \nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-5x>0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (5,\\infty ).$" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352314 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352315 13194139535500 true] [17592186352314 1089 "q-116189" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352314 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535500 true] [17592186352314 1100 "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x.$" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352314 1113 #uuid "6db048bd-fcb3-5632-92a9-6961f0b5074a" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352314 1145 "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2} & \\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x-\\frac{35}{2} & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+2x-35) & \\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x+7)(x-5) & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+7)(x-5) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+7)(x-5).$ The roots are $x=-7$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-7),$ $(-7,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-7)$ | $(-7,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+7$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+7)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+7)(x-5)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+7)(x-5)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-7)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-7$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{35}{2}\\ge \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}x$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [5,\\infty ).$" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352316 13194139535500 true] [17592186352315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352317 13194139535500 true] [17592186352315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352318 13194139535500 true] [17592186352315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352319 13194139535500 true] [17592186352315 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352320 13194139535500 true] [17592186352315 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535500 true] [17592186352315 1041 "selection" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352315 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352319 13194139535500 true] [17592186352315 1110 #uuid "fb3be6f4-6b46-5198-adc6-f1445f5f5f45" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352316 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [7,\\infty )" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352316 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352316 1111 #uuid "be5f2f80-fd83-5eed-b8b4-871bddf6ded1" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352317 1034 "x\\in [-5,7]" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352317 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352317 1111 #uuid "65013fd6-99f6-5f4e-8d39-ac190858fc54" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352318 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-5]\\cup [7,\\infty )" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352318 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352318 1111 #uuid "e5e99dc8-ae1d-5df7-81bc-d75b625d59aa" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352319 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-7]\\cup [5,\\infty )" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352319 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352319 1111 #uuid "6fb48cfe-0299-5b9e-b426-4a7d12119287" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352320 1034 "x\\in [-7,5]" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352320 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352320 1111 #uuid "e8d44521-7e93-5fe2-a4b1-57e13e30d769" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352321 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352322 13194139535500 true] [17592186352321 1089 "q-116194" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352321 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535500 true] [17592186352321 1100 "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2}.$" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352321 1113 #uuid "d79bf4af-b3e2-564d-8963-3b028b0571ca" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352321 1145 "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x & \\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+7x-4 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x+4)(2x-1) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+4)(2x-1).$ The roots are $x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2},$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-4),$ $(-4,\\frac{1}{2}),$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $(-4,\\frac{1}{2})$ | $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+4$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $2x-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+4)(2x-1)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+4)(2x-1)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+4)(2x-1)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-4$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-x\\ge 4-8x-{x}^{2}$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352323 13194139535500 true] [17592186352322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352324 13194139535500 true] [17592186352322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352325 13194139535500 true] [17592186352322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352326 13194139535500 true] [17592186352322 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352327 13194139535500 true] [17592186352322 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535500 true] [17592186352322 1041 "selection" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352322 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352324 13194139535500 true] [17592186352322 1110 #uuid "2da1890b-ab4c-5e5d-ac30-9808c228357a" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352323 1034 "x\\in [-\\frac{1}{2},4]" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352323 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352323 1111 #uuid "c7346db0-9dcd-5a2c-aea2-9aa9583e57e4" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352324 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352324 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352324 1111 #uuid "e27f4f2a-b397-5038-bb43-2808e6bed039" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352325 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]\\cup [4,\\infty )" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352325 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352325 1111 #uuid "4529660d-37f1-5916-af96-0b059d0c5a5b" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352326 1034 "x\\in [-4,\\frac{1}{2}]" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352326 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352326 1111 #uuid "01de2e5e-a5ac-5c6d-9ed9-bbe6a164b2d3" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352327 1034 "x\\in (-\\infty ,-4]\\cup [2,\\infty )" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352327 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352327 1111 #uuid "fb615cdf-4492-50de-9643-ff4c1ed02f24" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352328 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352329 13194139535500 true] [17592186352328 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352331 13194139535500 true] [17592186352328 1089 "q-277163" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352328 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535500 true] [17592186352328 1100 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+x-30<0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352328 1113 #uuid "58fd3bd3-662f-5eb8-98ba-b1ef6da33193" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352328 1145 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+x-30 & <0 \\\\ (x+6)(x-5) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+6)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=-6$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-6),$ $(-6,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-6)$ | $(-6,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+6$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+6)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+6)(x-5)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+6)(x-5)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-6,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+x-30<0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-6,5).\n$$" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352329 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352330 13194139535500 true] [17592186352329 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535500 true] [17592186352329 1041 "field-1" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352329 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352330 13194139535500 true] [17592186352329 1110 #uuid "f44dcd68-0fd8-5f38-9193-5b51a293ada4" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352330 1034 "-6" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352330 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352330 1111 #uuid "c34be5fa-342f-55f1-8010-fe2a311588d6" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352331 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352332 13194139535500 true] [17592186352331 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535500 true] [17592186352331 1041 "field-2" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352331 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352332 13194139535500 true] [17592186352331 1110 #uuid "ea5e474d-9c2e-5a9b-a21b-3c56587f6e5b" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352332 1034 "5" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352332 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352332 1111 #uuid "86413ed8-599d-5b1f-96ef-819d6b08d1f7" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352333 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352334 13194139535500 true] [17592186352333 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352336 13194139535500 true] [17592186352333 1089 "q-278314" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352333 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535500 true] [17592186352333 1100 "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-2x-3<0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352333 1113 #uuid "de8f525f-9eb7-5b74-8764-f14d21d4fb5c" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352333 1145 "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x-3 & <0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-3) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+1)(x-3)=0.$ The roots are $x=-1$ and $x=3,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,3),$ and $(3,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,3)$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-3$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+1)(x-3)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+1)(x-3)<0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+1)(x-3)$ is negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-1,3).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-2x-3<0$ for all $x\\in (-1,3).$" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352334 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352335 13194139535500 true] [17592186352334 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535500 true] [17592186352334 1041 "field-1" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352334 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352335 13194139535500 true] [17592186352334 1110 #uuid "39f5fa4f-4f72-5d73-84d5-210e7fd8eafe" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352335 1034 "-1" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352335 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535500 true] [17592186352335 1111 #uuid "71550b14-774c-5535-8d1e-41ee55757918" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352336 1000 #edb/ref 17592186352337 13194139535500 true] [17592186352336 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535500 true] [17592186352336 1041 "field-2" 13194139535500 true] [17592186352336 1104 #edb/ref 17592186352337 13194139535500 tr